# Overview
#
# Reports a word or a phrase that sells a feature instead of describing
# it (`seamless`, `powerful`, `battle-tested`). The word tells the
# reader that the feature is good and gives no fact to judge it by. The
# fix states the fact and lets the reader judge its weight. The rule
# sits at Recommended, because some of the words also carry a literal
# sense.
#
# Expression
#
# `main` lists each word and each phrase. A space in a pattern is a
# whitespace token and matches only whitespace. Each compound that
# writers also hyphenate therefore takes `[-, ( )]` between its words,
# so it matches `battle tested` and `battle-tested` alike.
#
# `robust` skips a following `to` or `against`. A negated word after a
# space needs a word to consume, so `robust` has a twin for the end of a
# clause (`robust!( )`). A clause is a subject and its verb, such as
# `the tests pass`.
#
# False Positives
#
# `robust` keeps a technical sense behind its guard. `robust to` names a
# statistical property (`robust to outliers`), and `robust against`
# names a fault that the code survives (`robust against wall clock
# jumps`).
#
# `first class` and `super` are left out, because each names something
# in code (a first-class function, a superclass call). `enduring` stays
# out too (`enduring storage outlives the pod`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# `highly` sits in the Recommended ProseIntensifier, because it raises
# an adjective instead of selling a feature.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 119 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 16 of 20 sampled
# hits sold a feature. The misfires used `comprehensive` for complete
# (`a comprehensive discovery document`) and `powerful` for privileged
# (`powerful pods`).
expr main [seamless, seamlessly, (robust ![to, against]), (robust!( )), powerful, elegant, elegantly, comprehensive, intuitive, effortless, effortlessly, blazing, blazingly, revolutionary, innovative, sophisticated, intricate, vibrant, (a breeze), holistic, groundbreaking, transformative, (ever[-, ( )]evolving), (rapidly evolving), (top[-, ( )]notch), (battle[-, ( )]tested), (production[-, ( )]ready), (enterprise[-, ( )]grade), (out[-, ( )]of[-, ( )]the[-, ( )]box), (bells and whistles), (feature[-, ( )]rich), (cutting[-, ( )]edge), (state[-, ( )]of[-, ( )]the[-, ( )]art), (lightning[-, ( )]fast), (best[-, ( )]in[-, ( )]class), (world[-, ( )]class), (next[-, ( )]generation), (game[-, ( )]changing), (game[-, ( )]changer), (game[-, ( )]changers)]
let message "Marketing language. State the fact and let the reader judge its weight."
let description "Flags marketing adjectives that inflate a claim without informing."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The bucket refills every second."
allows "The estimator is robust to outliers."
allows "The clock is robust against wall clock jumps."
allows "Go has first-class functions."
